WEBVTT

00:00.658 --> 00:02.324
- [Instructor] What we're
going to do in this video

00:02.324 --> 00:05.268
is try to find the
derivative with respect to X

00:05.268 --> 00:06.268
of X squared

00:07.925 --> 00:08.758
sin of X.

00:10.068 --> 00:12.359
All of that to the third power.

00:12.359 --> 00:13.882
And what's going to be
interesting is that there's

00:13.882 --> 00:15.505
multiple ways to tackle it.

00:15.505 --> 00:16.925
And I encourage you to pause the video

00:16.925 --> 00:20.341
and see if you could work
through it on your own.

00:20.341 --> 00:22.682
So there's actually multiple techniques.

00:22.682 --> 00:25.504
One path is to do the chain rule first.

00:25.504 --> 00:27.087
So I'll just say CR

00:28.464 --> 00:30.215
for chain rule first.

00:30.215 --> 00:33.005
And so I have, I'm taking the
derivative with respect to X

00:33.005 --> 00:35.442
of something to the third power.

00:35.442 --> 00:37.963
So, if I take the derivative it would be

00:37.963 --> 00:40.329
the derivative with
respect to that something.

00:40.329 --> 00:41.556
So that would be

00:41.556 --> 00:43.877
three times that something

00:43.877 --> 00:45.404
squared

00:45.404 --> 00:48.826
times the derivative with respect to X

00:48.826 --> 00:50.936
of that something.

00:50.936 --> 00:52.657
Where the something, in this case,

00:52.657 --> 00:54.490
is X squared sin of X.

00:56.901 --> 00:57.984
X squared sin

00:59.054 --> 00:59.932
of X.

00:59.932 --> 01:03.244
This is just an application
of the chain rule.

01:03.244 --> 01:06.143
Now, the second part, what would that be?

01:06.143 --> 01:07.707
The second part here

01:07.707 --> 01:09.518
do this in another color.

01:09.518 --> 01:10.884
In orange.

01:10.884 --> 01:13.225
Well here, I would apply the product rule.

01:13.225 --> 01:15.325
I have a product of two expressions.

01:15.325 --> 01:17.694
So I would take the derivative
of, let me write this down.

01:17.694 --> 01:20.913
So this is gonna be the product rule.

01:20.913 --> 01:21.996
Product rule.

01:22.983 --> 01:26.179
I would take the derivative
of the first expression.

01:26.179 --> 01:29.103
So, X, derivative of X squared

01:29.103 --> 01:30.544
is two X.

01:30.544 --> 01:32.280
Let me write a little bit to the right.

01:32.280 --> 01:33.437
This is gonna be two X

01:33.437 --> 01:34.873
times the second expression

01:34.873 --> 01:35.706
sin of X.

01:36.923 --> 01:39.795
Plus the first expression X squared

01:39.795 --> 01:41.695
times the derivative of the second one.

01:41.695 --> 01:42.662
Cosin of X.

01:42.662 --> 01:43.862
That's just the product rule

01:43.862 --> 01:45.685
as applied to this part

01:45.685 --> 01:47.292
right over here.

01:47.292 --> 01:49.289
And all of that, of course,
is being multiplied by

01:49.289 --> 01:50.802
this up front.

01:50.802 --> 01:52.460
Which actually, let me just

01:52.460 --> 01:54.120
rewrite that.

01:54.120 --> 01:57.581
So all of this I could rewrite as

01:57.581 --> 01:59.532
let's see, this would be

01:59.532 --> 02:00.449
three times

02:02.647 --> 02:04.848
if I have the product
raised to the second power

02:04.848 --> 02:06.500
I could take each of
them to the second power

02:06.500 --> 02:07.642
and then take their products.

02:07.642 --> 02:09.558
So X squared squared is

02:09.558 --> 02:11.321
X to the fourth.

02:11.321 --> 02:13.019
And then sin of X squared is

02:13.019 --> 02:14.415
sin

02:14.415 --> 02:16.023
squared of X.

02:16.023 --> 02:18.931
And then all of that is
being multiplied by that.

02:18.931 --> 02:22.151
And, if we want, we can
algebraically simplify.

02:22.151 --> 02:24.835
We can distribute everything out.

02:24.835 --> 02:27.581
In which case, what would we get?

02:27.581 --> 02:28.556
Well let's see.

02:28.556 --> 02:30.473
Three times two is six.

02:31.718 --> 02:33.698
X to the fourth times X is

02:33.698 --> 02:35.011
X to the fifth.

02:35.011 --> 02:36.856
Sin squared of X times sin of X

02:36.856 --> 02:37.773
is sin of X

02:38.910 --> 02:40.432
to the third power.

02:40.432 --> 02:42.595
And then, let's see, three

02:42.595 --> 02:45.742
so plus three, X to the
fourth times X squared is

02:45.742 --> 02:47.465
X to the sixth power.

02:47.465 --> 02:50.132
And then I have sin squared of X

02:50.132 --> 02:51.770
sin squared of X

02:51.770 --> 02:52.687
cosin of X.

02:53.528 --> 02:55.257
So there you have it, that's one strategy

02:55.257 --> 02:57.539
chain rule first, and then product rule.

02:57.539 --> 02:59.448
What would be another strategy

02:59.448 --> 03:02.098
pause the video and try to think of it.

03:02.098 --> 03:04.250
Well, we could just algebraically

03:04.250 --> 03:06.520
use our exponent properties first.

03:06.520 --> 03:09.313
In which case, this is
going to be equal to

03:09.313 --> 03:11.954
the derivative with respect to X

03:11.954 --> 03:12.787
of

03:14.618 --> 03:17.585
if I'm taking X squared times
sin of X to the third power

03:17.585 --> 03:19.584
instead I could say X to the third power

03:19.584 --> 03:22.986
which is going to be X to the sixth.

03:22.986 --> 03:24.860
And then sin of X to the third power.

03:24.860 --> 03:25.693
Sin of X

03:26.840 --> 03:27.947
to the

03:27.947 --> 03:28.849
third power.

03:28.849 --> 03:30.929
I'm using the same exponent property

03:30.929 --> 03:34.255
that we used right over
here to simplify this.

03:34.255 --> 03:36.334
If I have if I'm doing the product things

03:36.334 --> 03:38.396
to some exponent, well
that's the same thing

03:38.396 --> 03:40.012
if each of them raised to the exponent

03:40.012 --> 03:42.036
and then the product of the two.

03:42.036 --> 03:43.919
Now how would we tackle this?

03:43.919 --> 03:45.867
Well, I here, I would do

03:45.867 --> 03:47.970
the product rule first.

03:47.970 --> 03:49.337
So let's do that.

03:49.337 --> 03:51.379
So let's do the product

03:51.379 --> 03:52.638
rule.

03:52.638 --> 03:53.904
So, we're gonna take

03:53.904 --> 03:56.125
the derivative of the first expression.

03:56.125 --> 03:57.619
So, derivative of X to the sixth

03:57.619 --> 03:58.452
is six

03:59.508 --> 04:01.568
X to the fifth.

04:01.568 --> 04:03.053
Times the second expression.

04:03.053 --> 04:04.284
Sin

04:04.284 --> 04:06.076
to the third of X.

04:06.076 --> 04:07.921
Or, sin of X to the third power.

04:07.921 --> 04:10.754
Plus the first X to the sixth

04:10.754 --> 04:12.376
times the derivative of the second

04:12.376 --> 04:13.937
and I'm just gonna write that

04:13.937 --> 04:14.976
D DX of

04:14.976 --> 04:15.809
sin of X

04:17.576 --> 04:19.159
to the third power.

04:20.193 --> 04:23.342
Now, to evaluate this right over here

04:23.342 --> 04:26.125
it does definitely make sense

04:26.125 --> 04:28.650
to use the chain rule.

04:28.650 --> 04:30.298
Use

04:30.298 --> 04:31.695
the chain rule.

04:31.695 --> 04:34.224
And so what is this going to be?

04:34.224 --> 04:36.057
Well I have the derivative of something

04:36.057 --> 04:37.069
to the third power.

04:37.069 --> 04:39.086
So that's going to be three

04:39.086 --> 04:40.777
times that something squared

04:40.777 --> 04:43.751
times the derivative of that something.

04:43.751 --> 04:46.678
So in this case, the
something is sin of X.

04:46.678 --> 04:49.867
And the derivative of
sin of X is cosin of X.

04:49.867 --> 04:51.987
Then I have all of this
business over here.

04:51.987 --> 04:54.823
I have six X to the fifth.

04:54.823 --> 04:56.647
Sin to the third

04:56.647 --> 04:58.422
or sin of X to the third power.

04:58.422 --> 04:59.472
Plus

04:59.472 --> 05:00.722
X to the sixth.

05:02.179 --> 05:04.331
And if I were to just
simplify this a little bit

05:04.331 --> 05:05.798
in fact, you see it very clearly.

05:05.798 --> 05:08.467
These two things are equivalent.

05:08.467 --> 05:09.300
This

05:10.151 --> 05:12.402
this term is exactly
equivalent to this term

05:12.402 --> 05:13.413
the way it's written.

05:13.413 --> 05:15.545
And then this is exactly,

05:15.545 --> 05:18.399
if you multiply three times X to the sixth

05:18.399 --> 05:20.123
sin of X squared

05:20.123 --> 05:21.547
cosin of X.

05:21.547 --> 05:23.143
So, the nice thing about math

05:23.143 --> 05:24.982
if we're doing things
that make logical sense

05:24.982 --> 05:26.842
we should get to the same endpoint.

05:26.842 --> 05:29.583
But the point here is that
there's multiple strategies.

05:29.583 --> 05:30.940
You could use a chain rule first

05:30.940 --> 05:32.077
and then the product rule.

05:32.077 --> 05:33.378
Or you could use a product rule first,

05:33.378 --> 05:34.502
and then the chain rule.

05:34.502 --> 05:36.910
In this case, you could
debate which one is faster.

05:36.910 --> 05:38.040
It looks like the one on the right

05:38.040 --> 05:39.886
might be a little bit faster.

05:39.886 --> 05:40.956
But

05:40.956 --> 05:42.060
sometimes

05:42.060 --> 05:43.533
these two are pretty close.

05:43.533 --> 05:45.640
But sometimes it'll be more clear than not

05:45.640 --> 05:47.428
which one is preferable.

05:47.428 --> 05:49.588
You really want to minimize
the amount of hairiness,

05:49.588 --> 05:51.355
the amount the number of steps

05:51.355 --> 05:53.659
the chances for careless
mistakes you might have.
